<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andong</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andong is a city in North Gyeongsang province in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakdong-river">Nakdong River</a>. In 1361, when Red Turban rebels from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> took the Goryeo capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaegyong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongmin-of-goryeo">King Gongmin</a> took refuge there. Under Joseon it became a stronghold of Confucian scholarship, and its Hahoe Folk Village and Dosan Seowon academy are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage properties.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andong.jpg" alt="Andong" width="330" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Korea.net / Korean Culture and Information Service · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_bird's_eye_view_of_the_Hahoe_Folk_Village_(4458648859).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Andong is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-gyeongsang-province">North Gyeongsang Province</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> and the largest city in the northern part of the province. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakdong-river">Nakdong River</a> flows through it, and it is a market centre for the surrounding agricultural areas. It is known as a centre of culture and folk traditions, among them the Andong masks and the mask dance festival held each year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Around 1 BCE the town was founded by the Jinhan people and called Gochang. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a> the area was controlled by the kingdom of Silla. In 930 the Battle of Gochang was fought there between Hubaekje forces and the Goryeo army led by Wanggeon, who won control of the city and renamed it Andong.</p><p>Under the Joseon dynasty Andong became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> and was extremely conservative for a long time, producing many leading Confucian scholars. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yi-hwang">Yi Hwang</a> (1501–1570), one of the most prominent of all Korean scholars, came from Andong; he retired there late in life and began the Confucian academy Dosan Seowon, which was finished after his death. Its principal families, among them the Andong Kim, Andong Jang and Andong Kwon clans, were influential in Korean politics.</p><p>After the 16th century Andong became less influential until the early 19th century, when a marriage of the local Kim family brought strong influence over the royal family. The city was almost destroyed in intense fighting during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> in the early 1950s, but was quickly rebuilt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hahoe Folk Village, perhaps the most notable folk village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, was listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as a World Heritage site together with Yangdong Folk Village in 2010. Other seowon, or Confucian academies, include Byeongsan Seowon, which honours Yu Sŏngnyong, and Imcheon Seowon, for Kim Sŏngil. Andong's local foods include heotjesabap, a variety of bibimbap, Andong jjimdak, a chicken dish, and Andong soju, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> spirit made by traditional methods.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A yangban was expected to study the Confucian classics rather than work with his hands, and many lived on country estates as teachers and local leaders, as Yi Hwang did at his academy near <b>Andong</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/joseon">Social Hierarchy of Joseon Korea</a></li></ul></section>
